{
  "id": "COMPOUND:30040 - PROTEIN:5781",
  "verbalization": "Basiliskamide B is shown to interact with CMP-N-acetylneuraminate-beta-1,4-galactoside alpha-2,3-sialyltransferase, a protein that is targeted by multiple compounds, including (3aR,6S,6aR)-3,6-bis(3,4,5-trimethoxyphenyl)-1,3,3a,4,6,6a-hexahydrofuro[3,4-c]furan and 2-Methylheptyl propanoate. These compounds are both implicated in the regulation of sialylation, a process that plays a role in cellular signaling and immune function. The shared target of these molecules with CMP-N-acetylneuraminate-beta-1,4-galactoside alpha-2,3-sialyltransferase suggests a potential mechanism by which Basiliskamide B may exert its effects on A2780cisR, a cell line that is likely involved in the sialylation pathway. The evidence supports a mechanistic link between Basiliskamide B and A2780cisR through the inhibition of a key enzyme in the sialylation cascade, which could influence cellular processes and potentially lead to therapeutic effects. The consistent scores across the three paths indicate a robust and reliable relationship, though further validation with in vitro or in vivo experiments would be necessary to confirm the clinical relevance of this interaction.",
